<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Wu</h1><p class="st-meta">585 BCE – 473 BCE (approx.) · capital Gusu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The lower Yangtze kingdom that rose suddenly under Helü and Fuchai, sacked the Chu capital in 506 BCE and fell to its rival Yue in 473 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wu, also called Gouwu, claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-taibo">Taibo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongyong-of-wu">Zhongyong</a>, uncles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a>, who were said to have settled among the tattooed peoples of the south. It enters the record in 585 BCE under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoumeng">Shoumeng</a>, and Jin sent the Chu exile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-wuchen">Qu Wuchen</a> to teach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> warfare so that Wu would harass Chu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Wu's rulers called themselves kings and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Gusu</a> (Suzhou) amid lakes and rivers, fighting with fleets as well as armies and casting famous bronze swords. With the advisers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zixu">Wu Zixu</a> and, by tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu">Sun Tzu</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-helu-of-wu">Helü</a> captured the Chu capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ying-chu">Ying</a> in 506 BCE. King Fuchai defeated Yue, dug the Han Canal linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and the Huai, and claimed precedence over Jin at Huangchi in 482 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>While Fuchai was in the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goujian">King Goujian</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-yue">State of Yue</a> attacked. After a long siege Yue took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Gusu</a> in 473 BCE and Fuchai killed himself. Wu's story became a classic of Chinese storytelling about pride and revenge.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-wu.jpg" alt="State of Wu" width="320" height="217"><figcaption>Image: Yug · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_plain_5c._BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>585 BCE – 473 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gusu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shoumeng, Helü, Fuchai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gouwu, Wu (Zhou state)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-529&amp;focus=state-of-wu" aria-label="State of Wu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7795 1037 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Wu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8115 1293L8133 1338M8115 1293L7969 1681"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8133 1338h0M7969 1681h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8115 1293h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8115 1293h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-529&amp;focus=state-of-wu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

